**`psiCov` is positive semidefinite.** `psiCov = P[ψ_{θ₀}ψ_{θ₀}ᵀ]`
is a Gram-type matrix of `L²` functions, hence PSD (`t ⬝ᵥ psiCov *ᵥ t = P[⟪t, ψ⟫²] ≥ 0`;
`Matrix.posSemidef_gram`-flavored), providing the CLT assumption `hS_pos`.

entries.source:
/-- The **fixed-center shell** `M̄_δ = { ω ↦ m_θ ω − m_{θ₀} ω : ‖θ − θ₀‖ < δ }`. -/
def shellSet {d : ℕ} {Ω : Type*}
    (m : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) → Ω → ℝ) (θ₀ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) (δ : ℝ) :
    Set (Ω → ℝ) :=
  {g : Ω → ℝ | ∃ θ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d), ‖θ - θ₀‖ < δ ∧
    g = fun ω => m θ ω - m θ₀ ω}

/-- Squared pairwise differences generated by `F`.

This is the random-metric class used to replace empirical squared distances by
their population counterparts. Edge behavior: it is empty exactly when `F` is
empty. -/
def squareDifferenceClass (F : Set (Ω → ℝ)) : Set (Ω → ℝ) :=
  {h | ∃ f ∈ F, ∃ g ∈ F, h = fun x => (f x - g x) ^ 2}

/-- The strict subgraph of `f`, with the convention used in vdV's examples:
`(x,t)` belongs exactly when `t < f x`.

Edge behavior: strictness excludes points on the graph. This convention is
kept explicit because changing `<` to `≤` can alter exact finite traces. -/
def strictSubgraph (f : Ω → ℝ) : Set (Ω × ℝ) :=
  {p | p.2 < f p.1}

/-- The family of strict subgraphs generated by a function class.

Edge behavior: an empty function class generates the empty set family. -/
def strictSubgraphFamily (F : Set (Ω → ℝ)) : Set (Set (Ω × ℝ)) :=
  {A | ∃ f ∈ F, A = strictSubgraph f}

/-- The **truncated class**: every member of `G`, projected into `[−M, M]`. -/
def truncateClass (G : Set (Ω → ℝ)) (M : ℝ) : Set (Ω → ℝ) :=
  {g' | ∃ g ∈ G, g' = clampFn M g}

/-- The null distribution is a probability measure. -/
instance IsEquivariantInLaw.isProbabilityMeasure_nullDist
    {T : Kernel (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin k)) (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))}
    {A : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin k) ℝ}
    {S : Matrix (Fin k) (Fin k) ℝ}
    (h : IsEquivariantInLaw T A S) :
    IsProbabilityMeasure h.nullDist :=
  h.invariant_law.choose_spec.1

